/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For any molar property M of a mixture at fixed T and P, the property change of mixing is the mixture value minus the pure-component values weighted by mole fraction.
   - ΔMmix is the change per mole of mixture when pure components mix; M is the mixture molar property; xi is mole fraction of component i; Mipure is the molar property of pure i at the same T and P.
   - The same definition applies to U, H, S, G and V.
   - R is the gas constant, 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; xi are mole fractions; ln is natural logarithm.
   - T is absolute temperature in K.
   - A real mixture adds an excess part to each of these ideal changes.
   - An excess property is the real property change of mixing minus the ideal property change of mixing at the same T, P and composition.
   - ME is excess molar property; Mid is the molar property of an ideal solution at the same T, P and xi.
   - For enthalpy and volume, the ideal mixing terms are zero, so HE = ΔHmix and VE = ΔVmix.
   - For Gibbs energy the ideal term is not zero.
*/
